  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜まま (mama: as it is; unchanged)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: まま (mama) expresses a state that remains unchanged. Attach it after の (no) with nouns, or after the plain form of verbs/adjectives, to mean 'as it is' or 'remaining in that state.'\n\nExample from this song: あの頃のまま (ano koro no mama) = 'unchanged from those days' — the side profile in dreams remains exactly as it was back then\n\nMore examples:\n• ドアを開けたまま (doa o aketa mama, 'with the door left open') — from 開ける (akeru, 'to open')\n• このままでいい (kono mama de ii, 'it's fine as it is') — expressing contentment\n• 靴のまま入る (kutsu no mama hairu, 'enter with shoes on') — state unchanged\n\nNote: まま after a past-tense verb means something was left in that state. After の, it describes maintaining a noun's condition. It often implies the state was unexpected or noteworthy."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "あの頃 (ano koro, 'those days') + の (no) + まま (mama, 'as is') + で (de) → あの頃のままで (ano koro no mama de, 'unchanged from those days')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜てしまう (te shimau: end up doing; regrettably)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Attach しまう (shimau) to a verb's te-form to express completion with regret, or that something happened unintentionally. 'Ended up doing' or 'regrettably did.'\n\nExample from this song: 置いてきてしまった (oite kite shimatta) = 'ended up leaving behind' — from 置いてくる (oite kuru, 'to leave behind and come back') → 置いてきて (oite kite) + しまった (shimatta)\n\nMore examples:\n• 忘れてしまった (wasurete shimatta, 'unfortunately forgot') — from 忘れる (wasureru, 'to forget')\n• 食べてしまった (tabete shimatta, 'ended up eating it all') — from 食べる (taberu, 'to eat')\n• なくしてしまった (nakushite shimatta, 'lost it accidentally') — from なくす (nakusu, 'to lose')\n\nNote: Casual contractions: てしまう → ちゃう (chau), てしまった → ちゃった (chatta). Also でしまう → じゃう (jau)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "置いてくる (oite kuru, 'to leave behind') → 置いてきて (oite kite) → 置いてきてしまった (oite kite shimatta, 'ended up leaving behind')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜のなら (no nara: if it's the case that)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: のなら (no nara) combines the explanatory の (no) with the conditional なら (nara) to mean 'if it's the case that.' It adds an explanatory nuance to the condition.\n\nExample from this song: 取り戻せないのなら (torimodosenai no nara) = 'if it's the case that I can't get it back' — from 取り戻す (tomodosu, 'to get back') → 取り戻せない (torimodosenai, 'can't get back') + のなら\n\nMore examples:\n• 行くのなら早く (iku no nara hayaku, 'if you're going, go early') — from 行く (iku, 'to go')\n• 本当のなら信じる (hontou no nara shinjiru, 'if it's true, I'll believe it')\n• 会えるのなら会いたい (aeru no nara aitai, 'if I could meet you, I'd want to') — from 会える (aeru, 'can meet')\n\nNote: のなら is slightly more emphatic than just なら. It implies the speaker is responding to or considering a specific situation or piece of information."
      },
